VI  - 41
IP  - 3
DP  - 2013 Mar
TI  - Universal HIV testing in London tuberculosis clinics: a cluster randomised 
      controlled trial.
PG  - 627-34
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00034912 [doi]
AB  - We assessed whether implementation of a combination of interventions in London 
      tuberculosis clinics raised the levels of HIV test offers, acceptance and 
      coverage. A stepped-wedge cluster randomised controlled trial was conducted 
      across 24 clinics. Interventions were training of clinical staff and provision of 
      tailor-made information resources with or without a change in clinic policy from 
      selective to universal HIV testing. The primary outcome was HIV test acceptance 
      amongst those offered a test, before and after the intervention; the secondary 
      outcome was an offer of HIV testing. Additionally, the number and proportion of 
      HIV tests among all clinic attendees (coverage) was assessed. 1,315 patients were 
      seen in 24 clinics. The offer and coverage of testing rose significantly in 
      clinics without (p = 0.002 and p = 0.004, respectively) and with an existing 
      policy of universal testing (p = 0.02 and p = 0.04, respectively). However, the 
      level of HIV test acceptance did not increase in 18 clinics without routine 
      universal testing (p = 0.76) or the six clinics with existing universal testing 
      (p = 0.40). The intervention significantly increased the number of HIV tests 
      offered and proportion of participants tested, although acceptance did not change 
      significantly. However, the magnitude of increase is modest due to the high 
      baseline coverage.
