DP  - 2011 Dec
TI  - Efficacy of pleurodesis for malignant pleural effusions in breast cancer 
      patients.
PG  - 1425-30
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00171610 [doi]
AB  - This study retrospectively compared long-term outcomes between two groups of 
      breast cancer patients with malignant pleural effusion (MPE): those receiving 
      only systemic therapy (ST) and those receiving ST following initial pleurodesis 
      (P-ST). We identified 180 breast cancer patients from the National Cancer Center 
      Hospital (Tokyo, Japan) database who had received ST and P-ST as an initial 
      treatment for MPE between 1997 and 2008 for study inclusion. Pleural 
      progression-free survival (PPFS) was defined as the time from ST in the ST group 
      and from pleurodesis in the P-ST group to the first observation of pleural 
      progression or death from any cause. Of the 180 patients, 78 received ST and 102 
      received P-ST after MPE diagnosis. Median duration of follow-up was 12.7 months 
      (range 0.9-80.1 months). Median PPFS for the ST group and the P-ST group was 4.1 
      and 8.5 months, respectively. The difference in PPFS between the two groups was 
      statistically significant (p < 0.001) and the hazard ratio after adjusting for 
      prognostic factors in the P-ST group relative to the ST group was 0.24. Our 
      results suggest that the efficacy of P-ST may be superior to that of ST alone 
      with respect to local control of pleural effusions in breast cancer patients.
