TI  - Is air travel safe for those with lung disease?
PG  - 1057-63
AB  - Airlines commonly report respiratory in-flight emergencies; flight outcomes have 
      not been examined prospectively in large numbers of respiratory patients. The 
      current authors conducted a prospective, observational study of flight outcomes 
      in this group. UK respiratory specialists were invited to recruit patients 
      planning air travel. Centres undertook their usual pre-flight assessment. Within 
      2 weeks of returning, patients completed a questionnaire documenting symptoms, 
      in-flight oxygen use and unscheduled healthcare use. In total, 616 patients were 
      recruited. Of these, 500 (81%) returned questionnaires. The most common diagnoses 
      were airway (54%) and diffuse parenchymal lung disease (23%). In total, 12 
      patients died, seven before flying and five within 1 month. Pre-flight assessment 
      included oximetry (96%), spirometry (95%), hypoxic challenge (45%) and walk test 
      (10%). Of the patients, 11% did not fly. In those who flew, unscheduled 
      respiratory healthcare use increased from 9% in the 4 weeks prior to travel to 
      19% in the 4 weeks after travel. However, when compared with self-reported data 
      during the preceding year, medical consultations increased by just 2%. In 
      patients flying after careful respiratory specialist assessment, commercial air 
      travel appears generally safe.
