DP  - 2004 Nov
TI  - Morning levels of fibrinogen in children with sleep-disordered breathing.
PG  - 790-7
AB  - Elevated fibrinogen level is a predictor of cardiovascular disease in adults, and 
      it is associated with sleep-disordered breathing. Levels of fibrinogen in adults 
      are affected by other co-existing cardiovascular risk factors, which are not 
      usually present in children. To investigate the association between fibrinogen 
      and sleep-disordered breathing, a case-control study was carried out in children 
      with and without habitual snoring. All snoring children underwent 
      polysomnography. Morning fibrinogen values in 30 children with snoring and an 
      apnoea-hypopnoea index (AHI) > or =5 episodes x h(-1) (median (interquartile 
      range) 318 mg x dL(-1) (290-374)) were similar to values in 61 children with 
      snoring and an AHI <5 episodes x h(-1) (307 (269-346)). Both groups had higher 
      fibrinogen values than those in 23 controls without snoring (271 mg x dL(-1) 
      (244-294)). There was no correlation between fibrinogen values and AHI, 
      respiratory movement/arousal index or haemoglobin desaturation index. In 
      conclusion, fibrinogen values are higher in children with snoring than in 
      controls, but there is no association between these values and polysomnography 
      indices.
