VI  - 24
IP  - 5
DP  - 2004 Nov
TI  - Effect of in utero growth retardation on lung function at follow-up of 
      prematurely born infants.
PG  - 731-3
AB  - The aim of the study was to determine if prematurely born children who had 
      suffered intra-uterine growth retardation (IUGR) had more severe lung function 
      abnormalities than those born an appropriate weight for gestational age (AGA). 
      Analysis of the lung function results of 119 infants (median (range) gestational 
      age of 30 (23-35) weeks) was undertaken. In total, 31 of the infants had suffered 
      IUGR and were born small for gestational age (SGA). Functional residual capacity 
      and airways resistance (Raw) were measured at a median post-natal age of 10 
      (6-24) months. Specific airway conductance (sGaw) was calculated from thoracic 
      gas volume and Raw. The SGA children were born at a greater gestational age and 
      had a lower body weight at testing than the AGA children. Raw and sGaw differed 
      between the SGA and AGA children. Regression analysis demonstrated that lung 
      volumes were significantly related to body weight at testing, Raw was related to 
      IUGR, maternal smoking and bronchopulmonary dysplasia, and sGaw to maternal 
      smoking. In conclusion, these results suggest that prematurely born infants who 
      have suffered intra-uterine growth retardation may be at increased risk of 
      impaired lung function at follow-up.
