DP  - 2004 Apr
TI  - Respiratory changes during defecation in patients with chronic respiratory 
      failure.
PG  - 617-9
AB  - Oxygen (O2) desaturation may occur in patients affected by respiratory diseases 
      during daily activities, although most of these activities, e.g. walking, washing 
      and cooking, can be avoided or eventually performed with an external aid. In this 
      prospective study, the respiratory changes induced by the mandatory effort of 
      defecation were assessed in patients with chronic respiratory insufficiency. 
      Twenty-four consecutive patients with chronic respiratory failure due to 
      obstructive or restrictive pulmonary disorders, showing a marked O2 desaturation 
      during the 6-min walk test, were enrolled. Thirteen of them were already 
      established on long-term O2 therapy (LTOT), while 11 were not. O2 saturation 
      (Sa,O2), respiratory rate (RR), cardiac frequency (fC) and dyspnoea were measured 
      at rest, and during and after defecation. Sa,O2 decreased significantly during 
      defecation, while RR, fC and dyspnoea increased, both in the subgroup of patients 
      without significant resting hypoxaemia and in the subgroup of patients receiving 
      their usual resting flow of LTOT, as compared to resting values. In conclusion, 
      the respiratory system of patients with chronic respiratory failure may be 
      significantly strained by defecation.
