VI  - 20
IP  - 1
DP  - 2002 Jul
TI  - Relationship between exhaled air temperature and exhaled nitric oxide in 
      childhood asthma.
PG  - 108-11
AB  - Airway inflammation is a characteristic of asthma. Exhaled nitric oxide (eNO) has 
      been demonstrated to be related to actual levels of airway inflammation in 
      asthmatic patients. The purpose of this study was to investigate whether the 
      temperature of exhaled air is related to eNO levels. Temperature of exhaled air 
      and eNO were measured in 52 asthmatic children with a cross-sectional design. A 
      significant relationship was demonstrated between eNO and temperature of peak and 
      plateau exhaled air temperature. The relationship between both the peak and the 
      plateau values and eNO was more evident when it was corrected for environmental 
      temperature. These results suggest a relationship between exhaled nitric oxide 
      and the temperature of exhaled air in asthmatic patients not treated with 
      systemic steroids.
