VI  - 17
IP  - 6
DP  - 2001 Jun
TI  - Monitoring of nonlinear respiratory elastance using a multiple linear regression 
      analysis.
PG  - 1158-66
AB  - The elastic pressure/volume (P/V) curve obtained by the multiple linear 
      regression (MLR) technique using a new model, was compared with the quasi-static 
      P/V points obtained by the rapid airway occlusion technique. Seven infants were 
      studied during mechanical ventilation using a pressure controlled mode. The 
      resistive pressure was subtracted from airway opening pressure, thus determining 
      the elastance related pressure, which was then plotted against the volume to make 
      an MLR-elastance curve. Quasi-static P/V curves of the rapid occlusion technique 
      were constructed by plotting the different inspiratory and expiratory volumes 
      against the corresponding values of the quasi-static airway pressure. The 
      calculated MLR-elastance curves closely fit the experimental quasi-static P/V 
      points obtained by the occlusion technique. There were, however, some 
      discrepancies due to the viscoelastic behaviour of the respiratory system. 
      Although slightly altered by these discrepancies, the multiple linear 
      regression-elastance curves did fit the observed quasi-static pressure/volume 
      characteristics for use in clinical practice. The multiple linear regression 
      technique may prove to be clinically useful by continuous monitoring of 
      respiratory system mechanics during mechanical ventilation.
