TI  - Trends in the lifetime risk of COPD exacerbation requiring hospitalisation.
PG  - 964-71
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00129312 [doi]
AB  - This study aimed to estimate time trends in the lifetime risk of hospitalisation 
      with exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) in Denmark. 
      During the period from 1994 to 2008, a register-based cohort study was conducted 
      covering each subject in the entire population of Denmark (5.18 million in 2008). 
      Based on previously validated diagnosis codes, all COPD hospitalisations were 
      identified. Individual retrospective review periods of 8 years were used to 
      determine first-time hospitalisations. From year 2002 to 2008, all first-time 
      COPD hospitalised subjects aged ≥30 years were identified. The calculation of 
      lifetime risk was based on age- and sex-specific first-time COPD hospitalisation 
      rates and rates of COPD hospitalisation-free survival, assuming them to be 
      calendar time stationary. The study included 23.9 million person-years of risk 
      time and identified 48 959 first-time COPD hospitalisations. For 30-year-olds in 
      Denmark, the lifetime risk of COPD hospitalisation was 12.0% (95% CI 11.9-12.2) 
      for females and 10.9% (95% CI 10.8-11.1) for males. Trends were generally equal 
      in both sexes. During the period from 2002 to 2008, the rate of first-time COPD 
      hospitalisations decreased, while the survival of never COPD hospitalised 
      subjects increased. In consequence, the lifetime risk of COPD hospitalisation 
      remained constant.
