DP  - 2010 May
TI  - Home and workplace smoking bans in Italy, Ireland, Sweden, France and the Czech 
      Republic.
PG  - 969-79
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00066809 [doi]
AB  - The purpose of this study was to report predictors and prevalence of home and 
      workplace smoking bans in five European countries. We conducted a 
      population-based telephone survey of 4,977 females, ascertaining factors 
      associated with smoking bans. Odds ratios and 95% confidence intervals were 
      derived using unconditional logistic regression. A complete home smoking ban was 
      reported by 59.5% of French, 63.5% of Irish, 61.3% of Italian, 74.4% of Czech and 
      87.0% of Swedish females. Home smoking bans were associated with younger age and 
      being bothered by secondhand smoke, and among smokers, inversely associated with 
      greater tobacco dependence. Among nonsmokers, bans were also related to believing 
      smoking is harmful (OR 1.20, 95% CI 1.11-1.30) and having parents who smoke (OR 
      0.62, 95% CI 0.52-0.73). Workplace bans were reported by 92.6% of French, 96.5% 
      of Irish, 77.9% of Italian, 79.1% of Czech and 88.1% of Swedish females. 
      Workplace smoking bans were reported less often among those in technical 
      positions (OR 0.64, 95% CI 0.50-0.82) and among skilled workers (OR 0.53, 95% CI 
      0.32-0.88) than among professional workers. Workplace smoking bans are in place 
      for most workers in these countries. Having a home smoking ban was based on 
      smoking behaviour, demographics, beliefs and personal preference.
