VI  - 34
IP  - 3
DP  - 2009 Sep
TI  - CTLA4 gene polymorphisms are associated with chronic bronchitis.
PG  - 598-604
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00141808 [doi]
AB  - Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is characterised by chronic and 
      progressive dyspnoea, cough and sputum production. T-lymphocytes may play a key 
      role in the pathogenesis of COPD and chronic bronchitis. Cytotoxic T-lymphocyte 
      antigen (CTLA) 4 is a potential candidate gene because it modulates T-cell 
      activation. Genetic association between nine CTLA4 single nucleotide 
      polymorphisms (SNPs) and chronic bronchitis was assessed in 606 pedigrees (1,896 
      individuals) from the International COPD Genetics Network (ICGN) population. We 
      then replicated the associations in 342 COPD subjects with chronic bronchitis and 
      511 COPD subjects without chronic bronchitis from Bergen, Norway. Family-based 
      association tests were used to analyse the ICGN cohort, and a logistic regression 
      model was used for the Bergen cohort. Six CTLA4 SNPs were significantly 
      associated with chronic bronchitis in the ICGN cohort (0.0079< or = p < or 
      =0.0432), with three being replicated with the same directionality of association 
      in the Bergen cohort (0.0325< or = p < or =0.0408). One of these replicated SNPs 
      (rs231775) encodes the Thr to Ala substitution at amino acid position 17. 
      Haplotype analyses supported the results of single SNP analyses. Thus, CTLA4 is 
      likely to be a genetic determinant of chronic bronchitis among COPD cases.
