DP  - 2008 Mar
TI  - Mediastinal angiomyolipomas in a male patient affected by tuberous sclerosis.
PG  - 678-80
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00021207 [doi]
AB  - Classical angiomyolipomas are benign tumours composed of various tissues, 
      including components of fat, abnormal blood vessels and smooth muscle cells. They 
      are often found in association with tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC). The present 
      study reports a male patient affected by TSC with intermittent, massive chylous 
      pleural effusions, who developed recurrent mediastinal angiomyolipomas. The 
      tumours were characterised via histological and immunohistochemical methods. 
      Although angiomyolipomas frequently occur in the kidneys of TSC patients, this 
      case is the first report of mediastinal angiomyolipomas associated with TSC. 
      Besides lymphangioleiomyomatosis, this differential diagnosis has to be taken 
      into account in the case of chylous pleural effusions and mediastinal masses in 
      tuberous sclerosis complex patients.
