DP  - 2007 Jan
TI  - Genes, environments, development and asthma: a reappraisal.
PG  - 179-84
AB  - Significant advances have been made in our understanding of the role of genetic 
      variation in determining complex human phenotypes such as asthma. It is now well 
      established that there is no single "gene for asthma", in the way that the cystic 
      fibrosis transmembrane receptor is the "gene for cystic fibrosis". It is also 
      clear that among all genetic variants eventually found to be associated with 
      asthma, only a few will be replicated, and in the same direction, in the majority 
      of well-performed studies. Current evidence suggests that most asthma-related 
      polymorphisms determine risk for the disease in a context-dependent manner, i.e. 
      they interact with environmental factors, with polymorphisms in other genes and 
      with the specific developmental phase of the disease in which the association is 
      tested. Elucidating these complex interactions will allow us to understand better 
      the heterogeneity of the disease and thus to develop therapeutic tools tailored 
      to the specific form of the disease in each patient.
