VI  - 21
IP  - 3
DP  - 2003 Mar
TI  - The cost-effectiveness of nCPAP treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe 
      obstructive sleep apnoea.
PG  - 515-22
AB  - The demand for diagnostic and therapeutic services for obstructive sleep apnoea 
      syndrome (OSAS) showed marked growth during the 1990s. This paper analyses the 
      long-term cost-effectiveness of nasal continuous positive airway pressure (nCPAP) 
      treatment in comparison to conventional null treatment. A Markov model was used 
      to represent the natural history of OSAS based upon published evidence. Utility 
      values came from a survey of OSAS patients. Data on health costs were collected 
      from hospitals in the Basque Country, Spain. The incremental cost-effectiveness 
      ratio of nCPAP treatment is <6,000 Euros per quality-adjusted life year. On 
      disaggregated analysis, nCPAP treatment accounts for 86% of incremental costs; 
      84% of incremental effectiveness is attributable to improved quality of life. 
      Treatment of obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome with nasal continuous positive 
      airway pressure has a cost-effectiveness that is in line with that of other 
      commonly funded treatments such as antihypertensive drugs. The key clinical 
      benefit of nasal continuous positive airway pressure treatment is improvement in 
      the quality of life of patients with obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome. This 
      benefit is also precisely the one for which the evidence base is strongest. The 
      remaining uncertainties concerning the impact of nasal continuous positive airway 
      pressure on long-term mortality have only a relatively small impact on the 
      economics of treatment.
