DP  - 2013 Apr
TI  - The impact of preschool wheezing patterns on health-related quality of life at 
      age 4 years.
PG  - 952-9
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00015712 [doi]
AB  - We assessed whether dynamic preschool wheezing patterns affect health-related 
      quality of life (HRQOL) at age 4 years. The study included 3878 children 
      participating a prospective cohort study. Information on preschool wheezing was 
      obtained by questionnaire and children were categorised into: never, early, late 
      and persistent wheezing. At age 4 years HRQOL was measured using the Child Health 
      Questionnaire (CHQ). Persistent wheezing was associated with reduced scores for 
      nine out of 13 CHQ scales. No differences in psychosocial CHQ scores (p>0.05), 
      but lower physical CHQ scores were found in children with late and persistent 
      wheezing, compared to children who never wheezed (p<0.001). Mean scores on 
      general health perceptions were, respectively, eight and 12 points lower (on a 
      0-100 scale) in children with late and persistent wheezing (p<0.001), and 
      children with one to three episodes and four or more episodes of wheezing in the 
      fourth year respectively scored seven and 24 points lower (p<0.001), compared to 
      children who never wheezed. Persistent wheezing during preschool age 
      independently affects a child's HRQOL, particularly general health perceptions 
      and physical domains at age 4 years. HRQOL was more affected by frequent wheezing 
      episodes in the fourth year of life, rather than by the duration of wheezing at 
      age 0-4 years.
