VI  - 37
IP  - 6
DP  - 2011 Jun
TI  - Role of pulmonary infection in the development of chronic lung disease of 
      prematurity.
PG  - 1424-30
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00037810 [doi]
AB  - We studied the role of ante- and post-natal infection in the development of 
      chronic lung disease (CLD) of prematurity. 192 newborn infants (61 term and 131 
      pre-term of <34 weeks gestation: 88 with respiratory distress syndrome, 35 
      developed CLD and eight died) were recruited. 16S ribosomal RNA (rRNA) genes were 
      identified by PCR of DNA isolated from 840 gastric and lung fluid samples. 
      Ureaplasma spp. were also cultured. Presence of 16S rRNA genes (OR 1.6, 95% CI 
      1.2-2.2) and Ureaplasma spp. (OR 3.6, 95% CI 1.7-7.7) was significantly 
      associated with the development of CLD. This association remained if the 16S rRNA 
      genes and Ureaplasma spp. were first identified within the first 3 days of life 
      (OR 2.4 (95% CI 1.4-4.1) and 3.8 (95% CI 1.4-10.0), respectively) or if first 
      identified after 3 days of age (OR 1.7 (95% CI 1.1-2.8) and OR 5.1 (95% CI 
      1.3-19.8), respectively). Peak lung fluid interleukin (IL)-6 and IL-8 were 
      significantly associated with presence of microbes (p<0.0001 and p=0.0001, 
      respectively) and development of CLD (p=0.003 and 0.001, respectively). Both 
      early and late microbial presence in neonatal lung fluid samples was 
      significantly associated with the development of CLD suggesting that both ante- 
      and post-natal infection play a role in the development of CLD.
