VI  - 32
IP  - 6
DP  - 2008 Dec
TI  - Increased airway closure is a determinant of airway hyperresponsiveness.
PG  - 1563-9
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00114007 [doi]
AB  - In order to investigate whether increased airway closure is a component of airway 
      hyperresponsiveness (AHR), airway closure was compared during induced 
      bronchoconstriction in 62 asthmatic, 41 nonasthmatic nonobese (control) and 20 
      nonasthmatic obese (obese) subjects. Airway closure and airway narrowing were 
      measured by spirometry as percentage change in forced vital capacity (%DeltaFVC) 
      and change in forced expiratory ratio (DeltaFER), respectively. Multiple 
      regression analyses were used to assess the determinants of AHR, assessed by the 
      dose response slope (DRS). The DRS was significantly increased in asthmatics 
      compared with controls but did not differ between obese and controls. The 
      spirometric predictors of logDRS were baseline FER, DeltaFER, body mass index 
      (BMI) and %DeltaFVC. There was a negative relationship between BMI and logDRS in 
      the regression, suggesting a protective effect. The present findings suggest that 
      the extent of airway closure during induced bronchoconstriction is a determinant 
      of airway hyperresponsiveness, independent of the level of airway narrowing. 
      However, after adjusting for airway closure, obesity appears to protect against 
      airway hyperresponsiveness.
