VI  - 27
IP  - 6
DP  - 2006 Jun
TI  - Low-dose inhaled and nasal corticosteroid use and the risk of cataracts.
PG  - 1168-74
AB  - Orally inhaled corticosteroid use has been convincingly linked to an increase in 
      the risk of cataracts, although the risk at lower doses in common use remains 
      uncertain. The potential risk of cataracts with the use of nasal corticosteroids 
      is unknown. A matched nested case-control analysis was performed in a 
      population-based cohort of elderly people who had been dispensed medications for 
      airway disease, as identified through a universal drug benefit plan. Inhaled 
      corticosteroid use was associated with a dose-related increase in both the risk 
      of all cataracts and severe cataracts requiring extraction, and the increase in 
      risk of severe cataracts was apparent even at daily doses of </=500 microg. An 
      excess risk with nasal corticosteroids was not apparent for severe cataracts. It 
      is concluded that, among the elderly, even low doses of inhaled corticosteroids 
      are associated with a small but significant excess risk of cataracts requiring 
      extraction. Such an excess risk was not observed with nasal corticosteroids.
