VI  - 25
IP  - 5
DP  - 2005 May
TI  - Significance of positive Stenotrophomonas maltophilia culture in acute 
      respiratory tract infection.
PG  - 911-4
AB  - Stenotrophomonas maltophilia is a common coloniser of the respiratory tract of 
      patients with chronic lung disease, and, in the absence of pneumonia or 
      bacteraemia, is often ignored by physicians at the Royal Perth Hospital (Perth, 
      Australia). Experience at the Royal Perth Hospital was reviewed to determine 
      whether ignoring S. maltophilia in this setting has any apparent effect on 
      clinical outcome. All patients who presented with an acute respiratory illness 
      and yielded a positive culture for S. maltophilia between 1995 and 2002 were 
      retrospectively reviewed. All subjects had to yield a positive respiratory 
      isolate of S. maltophilia and undergo chest radiography within 24 h of the 
      isolate being obtained. Ninety-two episodes were identified in 89 individuals; 64 
      showed no evidence of consolidation. Of the study group, 51 (80.0%) received no 
      anti-S. maltophilia antibiotic therapy and 21 (32.8%) had a nosocomially acquired 
      isolate. The overall mortality rate was 20.3%. There was no impact of anti-S. 
      maltophilia therapy on outcome. The only independent predictor of mortality was 
      serum albumin level. As there was no measurable impact of antibiotic therapy, in 
      the absence of consolidation, a positive respiratory tract isolate of 
      Stenotrophomonas maltophilia probably represents colonisation of a severely 
      impaired host rather than invasive disease.
