VI  - 25
IP  - 4
DP  - 2005 Apr
TI  - Lung function, smoking and mortality in a 26-year follow-up of healthy 
      middle-aged males.
PG  - 618-25
AB  - Lung function has been associated with mortality after adjusting for other risk 
      factors; however, few studies have adjusted for physical fitness and reported 
      separate analyses according to smoking status. In 1972-1975, spirometry, clinical 
      and physiological parameters were recorded in 1,623 apparently healthy males aged 
      40-59 yrs. After 26 yrs of follow-up, the current authors investigated the 
      association between baseline lung function and mortality, adjusting for smoking, 
      physical fitness and other potential factors. By 2000, 615 individuals (38%) had 
      died, with 308 (50%) of these deaths from cardiovascular (CV) causes. Forced 
      expiratory volume in one second was a predictor of all-cause mortality (risk 
      ratio (RR) 1.10 per reduction of 10%) after adjusting for smoking, physical 
      fitness, age, systolic blood pressure, body mass index and serum cholesterol. The 
      corresponding multivariate RR was 1.07 for CV causes and 1.34 for respiratory 
      death. In conclusion, in stratified analyses among current and former smokers, 
      forced expiratory volume in one second % predicted was a strong independent 
      predictor of all-cause mortality and respiratory death among current smokers. 
      Forced expiratory volume in one second % predicted was not associated with 
      mortality among never-smokers.
