VI  - 24
IP  - 6
DP  - 2004 Dec
TI  - On the nature of Mycobacterium tuberculosis-latent bacilli.
PG  - 1044-51
AB  - Mycobacterium tuberculosis-latent bacilli are microorganisms that adapt to 
      stressful conditions generated by the infected host against them. By slowing 
      metabolism or becoming dormant, they may counterbalance these conditions and 
      appear as silent to the immune system. Moreover, the dynamic turnover of the 
      infected cells provokes a constant reactivation of the latent bacilli when the 
      environmental conditions are favourable, or an activation after being dormant in 
      necrotic and fibrotic lesions for a long period of time. Since there is no in 
      vivo nor in vitro evidence for quick resuscitation of dormant bacilli, the 
      current authors strongly favour the possibility that latent tuberculosis 
      infection can be maintained for no longer than approximately 10 yrs, which is, 
      nowadays, a time period very close to that considered for "primary" tuberculosis. 
      This concept may also be helpful for newer epidemiological considerations 
      regarding the real impact of reinfection in tuberculosis.
