DP  - 2001 Mar
TI  - X-linked agammaglobulinaemia and squamous lung cancer.
PG  - 570-2
AB  - A 32yr-old nonsmoking male, diagnosed as having X-linked agamma-globulinemia, 
      presented with fever, cough with purulent sputum, a very intense back pain and a 
      mass of 10 centimetres in lower left lobe. Diagnostic evaluation revealed a 
      squamous cell carcinoma with very aggressive metastases at L3. Malignancies are 
      the second leading cause of death in children and adults with congenital 
      immunodeficiency disorders, mostly non-Hodgkin lymphomas and gastric and colon 
      adenocarcinomas, but this is the first report of lung cancer in a patient with 
      X-linked agammaglobulinemia. Lung cancer incidence has been reported to be higher 
      in patients with other diseases of the lung, however, there is no clear evidence 
      of the role of bronchiectasis in developing lung cancer. It is possible that a 
      longer survival for patients with X-LA recently diagnosed, and an association of 
      chronic bronchial infection, could favour the development of pulmonary neoplasm.
