IS  - 0903-1936 (Print)
IS  - 0903-1936 (Linking)
VI  - 16
IP  - 2
DP  - 2000 Aug
TI  - Pulmonary artery pressure measurement during exercise testing in patients with 
      suspected pulmonary hypertension.
PG  - 282-7
AB  - It is recognized that exercise produces abnormally large increases in pulmonary 
      artery pressure in patients with pulmonary vascular disease as a consequence of a 
      variety of disorders, but the relationship between pressure and cardiopulmonary 
      exercise performance is poorly understood. This lack of understanding is due (in 
      part) to difficulty making measurements of pulmonary haemodynamics using 
      conventional fluid filled catheters. This article seeks to improve understanding 
      by comparing variables measured during formal exercise testing with simultaneous 
      measurements of pulmonary artery pressure using a micro-manometer tipped 
      catheter. Ten patients with suspected pulmonary hypertension were studied using a 
      micromanometer tipped pulmonary artery catheter, during cardiopulmonary exercise 
      testing. Ventilatory equivalents for oxygen and carbon dioxide correlated with 
      the pulmonary artery pressure measured on exercise, but oxygen pulse and oxygen 
      uptake did not. Ventilatory equivalents, noninvasively measured during exercise, 
      may merit further study as potential surrogates of pulmonary artery pressure and 
      hence be useful in identifying individuals at risk of developing pulmonary 
      hypertension.
