TI  - Resistin-like molecule-β is a human airway remodelling mediator.
PG  - 458-66
LID - 10.1183/09031936.00107811 [doi]
AB  - Though implicated in vascular remodelling, a role for the resistin-like molecule 
      (RELM)-β in human airway remodelling remains unexplored. We hypothesised that 
      RELM-β expression is increased in the airways of asthmatics and regulates airways 
      epithelial cell function. Expression of RELM-β in the bronchial mucosa and its 
      concentrations in bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid from asthmatics and controls 
      were measured by immunohistochemistry and ELISA, respectively. Proliferation 
      assays, Western blotting, ELISA and real-time PCR were employed to detect effects 
      of RELM-β on airways epithelial cells. RELM-β expression was increased in the 
      bronchial mucosa and BAL fluid of asthmatics compared with controls. In the 
      asthmatics, the numbers of mucosal RELM-β+ cells correlated inversely with forced 
      expiratory volume in 1 s (r=-0.531, p=0.016), while the numbers of epithelial 
      RELM-β+ cells correlated positively with those of mucin (MUC)5AC+ cells. In 
      vitro, interleukin-13 enhanced RELM-β expression by primary human airways 
      epithelial cells, while RELM-β itself acted on these cells to induce 
      proliferation, expression of MUC5AC, extracellular signal-regulated kinase 
      (ERK)/mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK)-phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase 
      (PI3K)/Akt phosphorylation and elevated expression of transforming growth 
      factor-β2, epidermal growth factor and vascular endothelial growth factor. RELM-β 
      has the potential to contribute to airway remodelling in diseases such as asthma 
      by acting on epithelial cells to increase proliferation, mucin and growth factor 
      production, at least partly via ERK/MAPK-PI3K/Akt signalling pathways.
