DP  - 2007 Apr
TI  - Travel to high altitude with pre-existing lung disease.
PG  - 770-92
AB  - The pathophysiology of high-altitude illnesses has been well studied in normal 
      individuals, but little is known about the risks of high-altitude travel in 
      patients with pre-existing lung disease. Although it would seem self-evident that 
      any patient with lung disease might not do well at high altitude, the type and 
      severity of disease will determine the likelihood of difficulty in a 
      high-altitude environment. The present review examines whether these individuals 
      are at risk of developing one of the main forms of acute or chronic high-altitude 
      illness and whether the underlying lung disease itself will get worse at high 
      elevations. Several groups of pulmonary disorders are considered, including 
      obstructive, restrictive, vascular, control of ventilation, pleural and 
      neuromuscular diseases. Attempts will be made to classify the risks faced by each 
      of these groups at high altitude and to provide recommendations regarding 
      evaluation prior to high-altitude travel, advice for or against taking such 
      excursions, and effective prophylactic measures.
