TI  - Detecting cardiac involvement in sarcoidosis: a call for prospective studies of 
      newer imaging techniques.
PG  - 418-22
AB  - Diagnosis of cardiac involvement in sarcoidosis is challenging and usually relies 
      on a combination of clinical findings and imaging abnormalities. The case of a 
      53-yr-old female is described who presented with ventricular tachycardia and 
      suspected angiosarcoma involving the right atrium and superior vena cava. A 
      combination of magnetic resonance imaging and 
      (18)F-2-fluoro-2-deoxyglucose-positron emission tomography were essential to the 
      diagnosis of cardiac sarcoidosis. Reversibility of the disease was predicted more 
      clearly by (18)F-2-fluoro-2-deoxyglucose-positron emission tomography than by 
      magnetic resonance imaging, and clinical activity was predicted by persistent 
      hypermetabolism on serial (18)F-2-fluoro-2-deoxyglucose-positron emission 
      tomography.
