TI  - Beyond the dogma: novel beta2-adrenoceptor signalling in the airways.
PG  - 1286-306
AB  - beta(2)-Adrenoceptor agonists evoke rapid bronchodilatation and are the mainstay 
      of the treatment of asthma symptoms worldwide. The mechanism of action of this 
      class of compounds is believed to involve the stimulation of adenylyl cyclase and 
      subsequent activation of the cyclic adenosine monosphosphate 
      (cAMP)/cAMP-dependent protein kinase cascade. This classical model of 
      beta(2)-adrenoceptor-mediated signal transduction is deeply entrenched, but there 
      is compelling evidence that agonism of beta(2)-adrenoceptors can lead to the 
      activation of multiple effector pathways, which now compels researchers in 
      academia and the pharmaceutical industry alike to think beyond the traditional 
      dogma. Therefore, the regulation by beta(2)-adrenoceptor agonists of responses, 
      including airways smooth muscle tone and the secretory capacity of the epithelium 
      and pro-inflammatory/immune cells, may be highly complex, involving both 
      cAMP-dependent and -independent mechanisms that, in many cases, may act in 
      concert. In this article, the current status of beta(2)-adrenoceptor-mediated 
      signalling in the airways is reviewed in the context of understanding mechanisms 
      that may underlie both the beneficial and detrimental effects of these drugs in 
      asthma symptom management.
