TI  - Endothelial interactions of neutrophils under flow in chronic obstructive 
      pulmonary disease.
PG  - 612-7
AB  - It is generally accepted that the neutrophil is central to the pathogenesis of 
      chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Enhanced endothelial interactions 
      of this cell may contribute to the susceptibility of smokers who develop the 
      disease; however, these interactions have not previously been studied in COPD. 
      The aim of the current study was to determine whether enhanced endothelial 
      interactions of neutrophils from smokers are a predisposing factor for the 
      development of COPD. Endothelial interactions under flow and adhesion molecule 
      expression of peripheral blood neutrophils were compared between seven 
      never-smokers (NS), seven healthy smokers (HS), 11 COPD patients with severe 
      alpha1-antitrypsin deficiency (PiZ) and neutrophils from 11 COPD patients without 
      the deficiency (PiM). Total adhesive and migratory responses (per mm2 endothelium 
      per 10(6) neutrophils) were significantly greater in the PiM group (mean+/-se 
      704.2+/-57.9 versus 509.3+/-48.8 in the PiZ group, 499.3+/-40.1 in the HS and 
      491.2+/-33.7 in the NS). This corresponded with increased macrophage antigen-1 
      (CD11b) expression on stimulated neutrophils in the PiM group compared with the 
      PiZ group (mean+/-se relative fluorescence intensity 1.4+/-0.1 versus 1.1+/-0.1). 
      In conclusion, the enhanced endothelial interaction of neutrophils from smokers 
      who have developed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease in the presence of 
      normal levels of alpha1-antitrypsin deficiency, but not in those with severe 
      alpha(1)-antitrypsin deficiency, suggests that this is a predisposing factor for 
      the development of the disease, and upregulation of macrophage antigen-1 may be 
      responsible.
