VI  - 21
IP  - 5
DP  - 2003 May
TI  - Spirometry in young children: should computer-animation programs be used during 
      testing?
PG  - 872-5
AB  - Currently, computer-animation programs are frequently used to instruct and 
      stimulate young children in performing maximal expiratory flow/volume (MEFV) 
      curves. The reproducibility and maximal performance of MEFV manoeuvres with and 
      without the use of two computer-animation programs (the "candles" and the 
      "balloon" programs) were evaluated. Eighty-eight children, aged 4-8 yrs, were 
      randomly assigned to one of the two animation programs. All children performed 
      two series of at least three technically acceptable curves, one series with the 
      incentive and one without, in random order. With the use of computer-animation 
      programs, a lower proportion of children were able to fulfil international 
      criteria for forced vital capacity (FVC) and forced expiratory volume in one 
      second (FEV1) reproducibility. The use of incentives improved reproducibility and 
      performance of peak expiratory flow (PEF). Performance of FVC decreased 
      significantly in 6-8-yr-old children using the animation programs. Training with 
      a program for a short period of time before the formal lung-function test may be 
      valuable. According to the results, however, the use of these programs during 
      tests under the guidance of an experienced lung-function technician cannot be 
      routinely recommended because of possible deteriorating effects on 
      reproducibility and performance of forced expiratory manoeuvres.
